#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124209 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12460" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b550d203e3be327eb2af27a1035b6693953dd2802bb203af06d5773e48d609c3.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y) = (0,-2),$ so the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2).$\n\n![](images/f8/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124210 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12461" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c6906429cf946f196b959cce7c20589b6212ae45df9607e15c6385be9aef858.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B,$ and $D.$ Hence, $A, B,$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/cd/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124211 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12465" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](images/5e/5e93d9b7357f877e4c14dd90aa70de484fda76fd38a18151b8f7af60b84cd80f.svg)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n+\n\\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/id #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7030" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function ![](images/10/10b37b962af60201173147106bc48c82b09570a76e2bc357ce777befddbf8d2c.svg) about ![](images/e2/e25dfc8ddd2dd59ae418293416501c482448efb030358a30e270072685040d19.svg) is given by\n![](images/2f/2fae448e0c5f617b794c07e38d2563343163226542206cc5062343c9acc7f5f4.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n![](images/5d/5dbf02ec5ec0392e52f35435112cc4d65e89639c6cddbc3f15730075af3e85d4.svg) \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ![](images/4f/4f21c39d3dd4745d253d1f7251d2329915074e377086b7afcfe2eb14ebd692f8.svg) and ![](images/86/86c9216a6929cd7fad43c29575aa4ac8255863ce6d1df24eee2830224659b9e4.svg)\n\nII. ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The point ![](images/1b/1b81872e7ebcaa9d0e229e5fbbe94b2a630d70400972d825cf8655961ffc7faa.svg) is a critical point of the function ![](images/35/35546b96dada825ebe9bf6a82803f85c40343497beb98e3b850067214a6e69ef.svg) if ![](images/6b/6bb3645c7e83b61a3945f1abd343897f1f70396e05762b9c80642a15f0d60387.svg) In such cases, we can classify ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) as follows:\n\n- if ![](images/3d/3da86e43bb3fd2af8d7a2b53852a22e8231436da92b2c50cb03120493a621ba0.svg) is positive definite, then ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](images/3d/3da86e43bb3fd2af8d7a2b53852a22e8231436da92b2c50cb03120493a621ba0.svg) is negative definite, then ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](images/3d/3da86e43bb3fd2af8d7a2b53852a22e8231436da92b2c50cb03120493a621ba0.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/30/303d917136470ad212a0f4ac83f6861e8435742c764d929f632d541e0761c02f.svg) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](images/c6/c6f4a56eed9073b3541e4398db9fc99f2e8ca94f651762e1f650bfe54865ca49.svg) for ![](images/16/1621ad9f5426c8fe19e2d5a00c5f92a60420aeaed7bfe623db70c9ab4e57527d.svg)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](images/b0/b0cdde792f80dc6b0c5defb2f8d767ae115ecf922fa46f2a233f10959e509870.svg) matrix ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](images/40/40138f7d0f5e38d137af53df63df0473cc827a5d8c8f957c7bd62d0ac2a30694.svg) (as well as the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](images/af/af25353ac60910a8cca7edfd3ea5312dea05646d41cd68c1cc45156c06391298.svg) we have ![](images/03/039aff55ff839029e398761cdb94f09b8c2203413637d6f41ca9c680659cc8d2.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is odd, and ![](images/54/54ac4b9f53d36b439544b913f7ba0dcfb153aa76b9b9624632a41cd9392ce5ac.svg) when ![](images/33/33f73a318016d781b6ae1aa913c2f1807ab5c7e6c2901e508fc56561fbe26b45.svg) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](images/0c/0cadce3122d5f1b1cb91927b6ab867404b826925c7a7900127b3824e7e286d2b.svg) but\n![](images/6e/6ee449b923787429cd51ad69f53e3420d1f50561840e54227a5cac671a7343cf.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](images/2b/2bfb4950b5dc2d95b9328d8d923b66cdc19ff1608394aff61fb1ae7811ec8d21.svg)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) is a saddle point of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(-1,-2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434670} {:answer/id #uuid "87e76412-257f-5583-a7e9-47e4ed2af022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(0,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434692} {:answer/id #uuid "acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434707} {:answer/id #uuid "d277550a-a25f-5521-be5b-be206e9c7195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434721} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7d1370-1b00-5b14-93b1-c569a931408e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434707} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e393d-2ed4-5869-b1f2-5c448966ff34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d4d9349-42bf-5237-9227-2e25538cab7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162705" :question/problem "![](images/89/897d0472039c499aee543a320f74b13bf1887f8b170ce1167a6c808d933ec676.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,2),$ so the global maximum point is $(1,2,2).$\n\n![](images/69/690d407982bfa9cde8fdce5b187006af1c24f9080a0cfea77e7551ca1d5cd940.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b674c8-eb57-5887-8426-8d408fa1479c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121518} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2a4026-9a30-57e8-a31c-3c06788c36b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121519} {:answer/id #uuid "207f2967-8496-5a9e-88f8-aba4ca44f2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121520} {:answer/id #uuid "528656e1-de5b-5574-9c6d-9c343b8f9305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121521} {:answer/id #uuid "58e91dd6-8a5c-5dac-bf79-fcf5d7a52091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e91dd6-8a5c-5dac-bf79-fcf5d7a52091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121522} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2522a01-cce7-51e3-a966-267ce3351d43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e025078b-b821-5afd-8d42-a7c7c420b244" :question/math-academy-id "q-163721" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =3-\\sqrt{5}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4ceb8c-14f0-56c6-8c95-f0ee15b4651f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121524} {:answer/id #uuid "eac39722-5b4b-5e3f-abe3-0ec8369122a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121525} {:answer/id #uuid "f4428592-ed70-51da-961c-e1f39f88ff4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121526} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8e477a-ee9a-5e8a-a0d8-7609b5654512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121527} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb59655-80cf-5782-9754-fdb5fa7a7b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eac39722-5b4b-5e3f-abe3-0ec8369122a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121525} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d8caccd-460a-5673-8d6f-d66dff746801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5496aa6-8f04-55cb-807e-38886a8c0e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-163722" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-12+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-24$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434666} {:answer/id #uuid "5d10f4aa-be44-5d96-9b85-bf9265aaedd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434679} {:answer/id #uuid "66cf0a35-8b8a-52ab-82bc-4a4518a010c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434683} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ac3574-a248-5137-9dca-b1453562f8cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434705} {:answer/id #uuid "cd819cdb-57a3-5138-b681-8a96c46bbbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270cb157-312b-598a-a963-8332f3594021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434666} :answer-field/id #uuid "6988e8b1-324d-5035-b234-84ec660debef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c7c764b-5efc-585f-b1d6-ab32ef1c26ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-163772" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434687} {:answer/id #uuid "ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434708} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434729} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/tex