ther positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434676} {:answer/id #uuid "63a1c505-6f99-5e6b-92df-f1c0f1d2c71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434681} {:answer/id #uuid "9984c603-9561-5d15-a7da-151b72f56966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434699} {:answer/id #uuid "d563919d-d0e0-5990-917f-9b961dfd1ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434723} {:answer/id #uuid "ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434728} :answer-field/id #uuid "812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163776" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1-\\sqrt{5}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =1+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460f604f-6eba-581b-8aa6-3280b9015d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434674} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0c4c69-837b-5418-a6bb-d90c5580a6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434690} {:answer/id #uuid "9816a77e-1d49-560e-9a4c-556c4c10f9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc20f8e-f720-5b88-9095-6a5461738176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434714} {:answer/id #uuid "f97cb8b8-e7b8-52f5-8999-c6136c919582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfc20f8e-f720-5b88-9095-6a5461738176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434714} :answer-field/id #uuid "873bc5fc-9412-54c7-80ae-349f7825de0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6771fc8-6834-5ff6-940e-46f1464b61f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163778" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=1$ and $det(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is positive-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=1$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=1>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1344f994-c7f3-5ec5-8bc3-dd5fb9f3b026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434660} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd164ff-c783-54c1-a435-26f55abca16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434700} {:answer/id #uuid "9be9a2ae-0d17-5397-99d7-af0f171bb5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434701} {:answer/id #uuid "a028efe8-76c4-5c04-82bd-e900a7c72295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434704} {:answer/id #uuid "f49e2cba-f810-5365-a372-e6f45bdb78dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1344f994-c7f3-5ec5-8bc3-dd5fb9f3b026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434660} :answer-field/id #uuid "aabf5329-c030-5348-b4b6-66587320416f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ec0f059-837e-5e3d-9bb4-8d292f2af4e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163781" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=8$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|=8>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434659} {:answer/id #uuid "42eede05-2998-52b8-82a7-f2c9485a49f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434672} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd13874-5f64-5852-a430-9a9d7ca920b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434685} {:answer/id #uuid "77644fed-85aa-50e4-ad64-f4f0dfaa2ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434686} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434726} :answer-field/id #uuid "a84e77c5-ac0f-5d82-92c5-daa66014b1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a2b77d8-21b3-512a-8f60-975fa8c9b29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163783" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-78+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,{\\lambda }_{2}=2-2\\sqrt{13}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2+2\\sqrt{13},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-6<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2-2\\sqrt{13}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =2+2\\sqrt{13}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d723f1a-3e00-5451-9a8e-3979cfbee414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434667} {:answer/id #uuid "439fd18d-a45e-5a4a-9276-7284619940e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434673} {:answer/id #uuid "65b7c2cb-305e-5629-b5ca-a76ce120a41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434682} {:answer/id #uuid "b4871234-59ca-5a03-86ce-df6b280edd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434710} {:answer/id #uuid "cb29e13c-7941-5abb-8dab-e750fa074891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d723f1a-3e00-5451-9a8e-3979cfbee414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434667} :answer-field/id #uuid "547254ce-982e-5bb1-87b7-5c56ae4ce713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65b2f5b4-89e0-551f-a08f-1b697ac55d76" :question/math-academy-id "q-163786" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=2$ and $det(A)=3$\n\nII. $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=2$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(2)-1(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b15bd6b-53d9-5f2c-a622-474fbfa83e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434661} {:answer/id #uuid "a7339b2b-0cd2-58b2-bb3e-d169ebbe1f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434706} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d79cac-7978-5207-995a-d295dee5e728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434712} {:answer/id #uuid "c1745eb2-62cc-52f0-8eb2-e37d4282fc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434715} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ed4b64-e1dd-5cfc-b0b1-4c2ef452329a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3ed4b64-e1dd-5cfc-b0b1-4c2ef452329a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434730} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f2c732-43a9-5f28-96fb-2493dab452dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5518b1c-8ee2-55b4-900e-3e5d5d3b8c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163788" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=-15$\n\nII. $A$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(3)-4(4)+2(-6) \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-19<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-31<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c750366-87fd-5df1-9550-7377df9d1451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433354} {:answer/id #uuid "48052d29-4422-5f9a-b0b0-95c416d880f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ and $(1,0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433357} {:answer/id #uuid "981d803b-2b71-589c-9dd7-89b262f35507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,-1)$ and $(2,-3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433362} {:answer/id #uuid "c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433365} {:answer/id #uuid "eedfc3db-8fe3-54f8-8907-da861cf5c513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ and $(-1,-2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1664007-041b-58fe-aa78-e1585e47911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433365} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e92c66f-883a-5be4-b481-34094dc1948a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccb6a2f-baf3-5789-838a-0e294ce49920" :question/math-academy-id "q-163794" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f82404522255a2e5b8be8b45d39062bd2239c15294024c02d6d4ad3dd3f44809.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.5.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum point is $(-1,2,-2.5).$\n\n![](images/29/29d2b703b9c17aa69bb41076e4bf40f509af4b44bd3ade14dbdeaec7cad25120.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dba2a64-ae52-5c4f-bd51-5ef13be3cb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434664} {:answer/id #uuid "4b93c0ca-42e8-5d66-981a-2e80551e5f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434677} {:answer/id #uuid "5730d517-7c69-5980-b411-3b3dad664998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434678} {:answer/id #uuid "94d307ae-d9c4-5198-958c-581b3e680cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434697} {:answer/id #uuid "b427e1ff-6b3d-5b12-b7a7-f23687f41949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b427e1ff-6b3d-5b12-b7a7-f23687f41949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434709} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f38069-1590-557e-918a-2e6f1810787d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e313337f-f7bb-58d5-96bd-6538b4821d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163835" :question/problem "![](images/58/58898653e07df36c7de1eac79aa9ce012e871c5323a344381645333eb1695adb.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.5.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.5.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(0,-2)$ and $(x,y)=(-2,1).$\n\n![](images/4b/4b8b56801adbffd5dd65b6e110593fdc0141b70315306c67ec7ed5842ae7f7d9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41662308-758c-57f7-94d5-3b0fe16cf47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434671} {:answer/id #uuid "880d681f-4e87-56e3-b724-e08426dfd46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434693} {:answer/id #uuid "8c693c22-c800-52a4-9e88-159bdde7ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434694} {:answer/id #uuid "c821506c-1b26-5aad-87f9-efd4a40384ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434717} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3551a0-fc31-5428-8862-90ab0efeebcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c821506c-1b26-5aad-87f9-efd4a40384ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434717} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c4ceca-3df8-5383-9289-174b40f5a7c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "931e8538-9965-59c5-8971-be8d18bbaef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163837" :question/problem "![](images/08/081dab1fcd5ccde5281ec4369417c4b0911a404695e9d147b046daf68f8621a6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global minimum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-5.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-5.$ The function reaches this value at   $(x,y)=(0,3).$\n\n![](images/c1/c1fb4d47cc10c74537e01803a633fd9f8d6c9b1c6f8b8cd945bfba058861c2d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a606ca6-3907-55d4-bc5d-c66042100c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281782} {:answer/id #uuid "3a753385-0a8a-5dfe-bed7-80c60cf215cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282204} {:answer/id #uuid "7feb410e-98b1-5206-95a7-24a5cc00115e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282789} {:answer/id #uuid "a69082ce-57b6-51d8-9d0d-481977368dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283138} {:answer/id #uuid "c93a332b-7da6-520a-a57f-2512abf83643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a753385-0a8a-5dfe-bed7-80c60cf215cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282204} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db6d840-3b00-5182-9642-94796d437bc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "999c6d8f-f6eb-52c5-981d-e4dac50005ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-163838" :question/problem "![](images/78/784f2cbc652df83239e40dbe830675a4c3ce99c9f51dec913ff2ec1f62612cdc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above.\nWhat is the global maximum of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y)=(-3,2).$\n\n![](images/39/39b694d4ea57e59d97102963009322aeef84d5fa58df07dbc854a73972ad7458.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "479b6949-d768-5a91-943c-b362a5bb5ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434675} {:answer/id #uuid "8c979729-0c50-52fc-93ef-b13c63005c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434695} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9c4318-1e04-56fd-b1b0-d959a8de76d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434702} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9af5d3-d4aa-5e2f-8e3a-f1745f0df43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $C,$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434703} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f6e322-f58a-582d-ac9a-02e88741ca94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "479b6949-d768-5a91-943c-b362a5bb5ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434675} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddd2fb64-7d5d-5d61-9a82-d15eb97782df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-164101" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d6a8563-51e7-5f75-b1f9-5030c16e40a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434680} {:answer/id #uuid "671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434684} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb013d8-4bec-59b6-be78-16c6b4278be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function does not have local minima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434688} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec46caa-3ab4-5a82-8cef-51349b889f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434689} {:answer/id #uuid "c579e011-3bca-5b23-92a7-a4c2a45142bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434684} :answer-field/id #uuid "caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-164151" :question/problem "![](i